00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:14.610
Imagine a $2,000 car… or a $100 laptop…

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:21.160
or a $70 iPhone… or imagine any product
ten times cheaper than it was.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:24.369
Imagine the fundamental market change that
would bring.

00:00:24.369 --> 00:00:30.170
Imagine the amount of demand there would be
for that $2,000 car or $100 laptop or $70

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:31.170
iPhone.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:37.050
That’s what Elon Musk imagined 15 years
ago when he sat on a pile of $165 million

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:38.079
dollars.

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:43.399
Elon Musk’s businesses are all centered
around some the most basic principles of economics

00:00:43.399 --> 00:00:44.399
out there.

00:00:44.399 --> 00:00:48.620
When he starts a business, he’s not necessarily
trying to do something new, he’s trying

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:50.309
to do something right.

00:00:50.309 --> 00:00:53.700
Musk made most of his early fortune through
his involvement with PayPal.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:59.289
In 1999 he founded a company called X.com
which was quickly bought by Confinity—the

00:00:59.289 --> 00:01:05.680
creators of PayPal—so when PayPal was bought
by eBay in 2002, Musk’s 11.7% ownership

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.380
of the company translated to $165 million
dollars.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:16.360
Elon Musk has always been deeply passionate
about space exploration and, as anyone knows,

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.810
public interest in space has been falling
since the Apollo era.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:24.530
Therefore, Musk’s plan with his newfound
fortune was to launch a rocket to mars carrying

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:29.210
a small greenhouse that would grow plants
on the surface of the red planet.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:33.310
Basically, he wanted to take all his money
and put it into a big publicity stunt for

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:34.900
space exploration.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:37.840
But he had a problem—it was too expensive.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:43.880
The cost of launches was absolutely immense
and, even when Musk tried to buy decommissioned

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:49.090
Russian ICBM’s, he couldn’t find a way
to pull off the project, but he had discovered

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:50.090
something.

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:52.320
The space launch industry was ripe for disruption.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.690
Here’s Joseph, the economics expert from
Real Life Lore to explain why.

00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:01.220
“The rocket development and space-launch
companies before Space X were essentially

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:02.240
aggregators.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.070
They bought engines and guidance systems and
all the other various components from other

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:09.310
companies to cobble together one completed
rocket.

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:13.330
But all the different component suppliers
also had their own component suppliers to

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:14.719
make their product.

00:02:14.719 --> 00:02:19.540
The suppliers of the suppliers not only had
to cover their development and manufacturing

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:23.799
costs, they had to sell their components at
a markup in order to make a profit, and then

00:02:23.799 --> 00:02:27.400
the next component manufacturer had to do
the same which means that by the time the

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:31.659
component gets to the company assembling the
rocket, it’s expensive.

00:02:31.659 --> 00:02:36.219
Not only that, but the assembly company also
has to pay for employees that work to actually

00:02:36.219 --> 00:02:39.769
figure out how to make all of the different
pieces work together.

00:02:39.769 --> 00:02:41.840
SpaceX however, works differently.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:47.779
It makes 85% of the components it uses itself,
which allows it to make cheaper parts.

00:02:47.779 --> 00:02:53.719
For example, if SpaceX had bought their radios
externally they would be paying $50,000-100,000

00:02:53.719 --> 00:02:59.650
dollars each, but since they develop them
internally they only cost $5,000 each to build,

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:02.529
a dramatic improvement in reducing cost.”

00:03:02.529 --> 00:03:06.489
Joseph from Real Life Lore has a brand new
book which includes two fantastic chapters

00:03:06.489 --> 00:03:11.099
explaining simple economic concepts like this
that I’ll link in the description, but let’s

00:03:11.099 --> 00:03:12.290
talk Tesla.

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:16.109
Tesla’s economic strategy is fairly similar
to SpaceX’s.

00:03:16.109 --> 00:03:22.870
Tesla themselves makes about 80% of the 5,300
parts in a Tesla car, but for the most part

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:26.919
they don’t make these, the batteries, at
least yet.

00:03:26.919 --> 00:03:32.069
Batteries are very difficult to make at a
competitive price so very few companies do.

00:03:32.069 --> 00:03:38.730
The largest three manufacturers—Panasonic,
BYD, and LG Chem—make a combined 63% of

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:39.999
the world’s batteries.

00:03:39.999 --> 00:03:44.700
Tesla, therefore, has historically just bought
batteries from Panasonic at a cost of about

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:46.739
$200 per kWh.

00:03:46.739 --> 00:03:53.769
But that means that Tesla’s smallest battery
pack, the 50 kWh version, costs $10,000 dollars

00:03:53.769 --> 00:03:55.400
just in components.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:04:00.619
When you’re trying to sell a $35,000 dollar
car and make a profit, that’s a significant

00:04:00.619 --> 00:04:02.659
cost that can be reduced.

00:04:02.659 --> 00:04:07.700
Therefore, Tesla is attempting to reduce the
cost of their batteries by 30% by building

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:11.099
their own factory in a joint-venture with
Panasonic.

00:04:11.099 --> 00:04:16.930
Their long-term goal, however, is to drop
the battery price below $100 dollars per kWh

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:21.919
which would either double the range or halve
the price of that 50 kWh battery pack.

00:04:21.919 --> 00:04:26.290
But the vertical integration of Tesla and
SpaceX isn’t all useful.

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:31.270
The companies basically have to learn and
perfect each step in the manufacturing process

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:35.599
and, if one step isn’t working, no cars
get made.

00:04:35.599 --> 00:04:41.400
For example, the Tesla Model 3, the low-cost
Tesla, is built using steel instead of aluminum

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.229
like the Model S and X.

00:04:43.229 --> 00:04:47.820
With this change, the manufacturer is having
troubles properly welding the vehicle bodies

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:52.760
together and so the entire production line
is slowed down massively.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:56.759
But there's something else unique about SpaceX
and Tesla’s production lines—they’re

00:04:56.759 --> 00:04:57.990
in the US.

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:01.850
Now this probably seems counterintuitive—why
would you put the production lines of two

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:06.240
companies working to make the least expensive
products on the market in one of the most

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.650
expensive labor markets in the world?

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:13.469
Almost every US company has relocated their
production lines to cheaper labor markets

00:05:13.469 --> 00:05:18.380
in Asia and Africa but Musk has always had
his in the US.

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:21.050
Believe it or not, this isn’t a PR move.

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:23.909
It actually makes sense for the two companies.

00:05:23.909 --> 00:05:28.599
Tesla and SpaceX’s production processes
are constantly being tweaked and optimized

00:05:28.599 --> 00:05:31.229
as the companies learn to make their products.

00:05:31.229 --> 00:05:36.300
While China might be able to build Tesla cars
at the same price by using cheaper human labor,

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:41.780
Tesla’s US factory is just miles away from
its headquarters in Palo Alto meaning that

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:47.090
the executive, development, and production
staff are all heavily integrated and can make

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:48.840
changes fast.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:51.000
SpaceX even takes this a step further.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.030
It’s offices and manufacturing lines are
all under one roof.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:06:00.550
The Tesla factory in particular is also heavily
automatized and the US excels in production

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:05.370
line automation with its abundance of highly
skilled workers, but just how much is Musk

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:07.460
dropping the price on his products?

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:11.880
The United Launch Alliance, which historically
has won most of the highly lucrative US government

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:17.719
launch contracts, is believed to charge more
than $400 million dollars all-in for a military

00:06:17.719 --> 00:06:21.580
satellite launch while SpaceX charges about
$80 million dollars.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:27.270
So, SpaceX is already at a fifth of the price,
but as mentioned, Elon Musk wants that to

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:28.580
fall to a tenth.

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:34.190
Here’s the key for that—the fuel used
in the Falcon 9 rocket only costs about $200,000

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:38.569
dollars per launch—it’s practically a
non-factor in the launch price.

00:06:38.569 --> 00:06:43.999
The real cost is of the rockets themselves,
so that’s why SpaceX is making them reusable.

00:06:43.999 --> 00:06:48.210
The first stage of the rocket is now being
designed to land back on earth and be put

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:51.419
back into service with dozens more launches.

00:06:51.419 --> 00:06:55.490
Once this system becomes reliable, it’s
believed that the cost savings will drop the

00:06:55.490 --> 00:07:00.800
launch price to $40 million dollars—a full
10 times cheaper than United Launch Alliance’s

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:02.189
military launch price.

00:07:02.189 --> 00:07:07.460
SpaceX’s long-term goal is to get the launch
price down to about $10 million dollars per

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:08.460
launch.

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:12.800
While the company has already made a significant
impact on the space industry, a launch price

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:17.320
as low as this would fundamentally change
what’s possible in space.

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:21.590
Real space tourism would become feasible,
commercial satellites would become downright

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:25.129
commonplace, and Space would become closer
than it’s ever been.

00:07:25.129 --> 00:07:30.430
But SpaceX does have a bit of a problem—people
aren’t really buying more rocket launches

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:32.150
even though prices are down.

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:35.270
It’s what’s known as a price inelastic
market.

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:40.000
That’s the opposite of Tesla and the electric
vehicle market where lower prices lead to

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:42.419
huge increases in sales.

00:07:42.419 --> 00:07:46.919
The problem with the space launch market is
that it is not a consumer market—normal

00:07:46.919 --> 00:07:49.309
people don’t buy rocket launches.

00:07:49.309 --> 00:07:53.360
Governments buy rocket launches and they don’t
care about price nearly as much as people

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:55.530
since it’s not the decision makers’ money.

00:07:55.530 --> 00:08:00.050
The US Air Force, for example, decides they
need to launch a certain number of satellites

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:04.650
each year for the national security reasons
and they’ll pay whatever it takes.

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:10.470
But Elon Musk’s life goal is to get humanity
to Mars—that’s why SpaceX exists—and

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:12.199
he needs money to do it.

00:08:12.199 --> 00:08:13.199
Lots of money.

00:08:13.199 --> 00:08:16.569
So, SpaceX is getting into the internet business.

00:08:16.569 --> 00:08:21.120
The company is actively developing a satellite
constellation that would provide high-speed

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:23.279
internet to anywhere on earth.

00:08:23.279 --> 00:08:28.289
Thousands of small satellites would be put
into low earth orbit and then anyone worldwide

00:08:28.289 --> 00:08:31.810
could hook into the network using an inexpensive
ground receiver.

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:38.199
If SpaceX got just 50 million users out of
the 7 billion in its proposed service area,

00:08:38.199 --> 00:08:41.969
this business could bring in $30 billion dollars
a year.

00:08:41.969 --> 00:08:46.110
Since SpaceX would be building and launching
the satellites themselves, costs would be

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:49.050
dramatically lower than the competition’s.

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:55.940
The whole commercial aspect of SpaceX essentially
exists to fund Musk’s future space exploration

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:56.940
projects.

00:08:56.940 --> 00:09:00.560
For that reason, SpaceX is not a public company
like Tesla.

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:05.690
Elon Musk does not want to make money with
SpaceX, he wants to get to Mars.

00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:09.570
He does not want to be beholden to shareholders
and profitability.

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:14.460
Musk has therefore publicly said that SpaceX
will not go public until the company achieves

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:19.180
regular flights to and from mars and thanks
to the entrepreneur’s understanding of basic

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:22.380
economics, that might not be too far off.

